-- dispatch: two evidence slots of ONE instruction that EACH nest, and the
-- pre-order the flat site list has to be in
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §7.2, §7.3, §8.2, A.68).
--
-- `NestedEvidenceIndices` is one slot nesting four deep, which the running
-- cursor of A.68 numbers correctly by accident: a single chain has no
-- sibling to be confused with. This is the shape that does. The cursor runs
-- in ALLOCATION order, and allocation is breadth-first — one instantiation
-- numbers ALL of its own slots before any of them is discharged — so `two`
-- numbered `a`'s element AFTER `b`, and `Lower.evidenceArguments`, which
-- reads the flat list as a pre-order tree, then took it for `a`'s
-- grandchild. `pair [ [ 1 ] ] [ [ 2 ] ]` compiled to a four-deep `List$eq`
-- for `a` and a bare `strict_eq` for `b`, exit 0, and `run/TwoSlotsNested`
-- is what that does to the answers.
--
-- So the ORDER of these rows is the tree and the `evidence_index` column is
-- only the identity the checker's two deduplicating tables key on
-- (`Solve.joinConstraint`, `Solve.appendSite`). Reading a golden here means
-- reading down the rows: a target takes the slots that follow it, one per
-- evidence parameter of its own.
--
-- `two` is the repro. `middle` is three slots where the MIDDLE one nests
-- three deep, so the walk has to come back out of a subtree to a sibling
-- rather than only descend — its rows run 0, 3, 1, 4, 6, 7, 2, 5, which is
-- the breadth-first numbering read depth-first. `tupled` is the other
-- half: slot 1 is a tuple of two nested lists, whose evidence rides on the
-- TARGET's `part` tree (§7.1) and not on the site list at all, so slot 0's
-- child must still land between the two — before the fix it landed after,
-- and the tuple's `eq` was handed to `List$eq` as the element comparison of
-- `[ 1 ]`.
pub pair : a, b -> Bool
    where
        a.eq : a, a -> Bool
        , b.eq : b, b -> Bool
pair x y =
    x == x && y == y


pub three : a, b, c -> Bool
    where
        a.eq : a, a -> Bool
        , b.eq : b, b -> Bool
        , c.eq : c, c -> Bool
three x y z =
    x == x && y == y && z == z


pub two : Bool
two =
    pair [ [ 1 ] ] [ [ 2 ] ]


pub middle : Bool
middle =
    three [ 1 ] [ [ [ 2 ] ] ] [ 3 ]


pub tupled : Bool
tupled =
    pair [ 1 ] ( [ [ 2 ] ], [ [ 3 ] ] )
